    body: `<p>They sit in the pew on Sunday morning, close enough that their shoulders touch. They look, to anyone watching, like a couple. And they are a couple — legally, domestically, in every external measure. They share a mortgage and a minivan and a last name. But they have not touched each other in months. Some of them have not touched each other in years. And neither of them knows how to talk about it — not to each other, not to their friends, and certainly not to their pastor.</p>

<p>The sexless marriage is one of the most common and least discussed realities in the American church. Researchers define it clinically — fewer than ten sexual encounters per year — but the clinical definition misses the lived experience, which is not primarily about frequency but about rejection, longing, shame, and the slow death of something that was supposed to be a gift.</p>

<p>The church has two modes when it comes to sex in marriage: celebration and prohibition. Before marriage, sex is forbidden. After marriage, sex is a blessing, a gift, a sacred act of union. The wedding night is the great transition — from no to yes, from sin to sacrament. But the church has almost nothing to say about the marriage in which the yes never came, or came and then stopped, or came with so much complication that it barely felt like a gift at all.</p>

<p>This silence has consequences. The spouse who desires physical intimacy and does not receive it carries a shame that has no outlet. The spouse who cannot offer physical intimacy — for reasons that range from trauma to medication to simple loss of desire — carries a guilt that has no relief. And between them, the silence grows, filling the space where honesty should be.</p>

<h2>What We Refuse to Name</h2>

<p>The first thing the church must do is name the problem honestly. A sexless marriage is not a minor inconvenience. It is a significant deprivation — for both spouses, regardless of who desires more and who desires less. Paul wrote to the Corinthians that married couples should not deprive one another except by mutual consent for a limited time. The church has used this passage to guilt reluctant spouses into compliance, which is a grotesque misreading. But the passage contains a genuine insight: physical intimacy in marriage is not optional. It is constitutive. It is part of what makes a marriage a marriage rather than a partnership or a friendship or a cohabitation arrangement.</p>

<p>This does not mean that every spouse is entitled to sex on demand. It means that both spouses are entitled to a marriage in which physical intimacy is present, valued, and mutually sought — even if the seeking is difficult, even if the intimacy looks different from what either person imagined, even if the path to it requires more honesty than either person wants to offer.</p>

<p>The reluctant spouse is not the villain. The desiring spouse is not the victim. These are two people caught in a dynamic that neither of them chose and neither of them knows how to change. The church's job is not to assign blame. It is to provide a framework — theological, pastoral, practical — for two people to talk about the most intimate dimension of their life together without shame.</p>

<h2>The Roots of Sexual Withdrawal</h2>

<p>Sexual withdrawal in marriage has causes, and the causes matter. They are not excuses. They are explanations, and explanations are the beginning of change.</p>

<p>For some spouses, the withdrawal is rooted in trauma. Sexual abuse in childhood, assault in adolescence, a previous relationship in which sex was coerced or punishing — these experiences do not disappear at the altar. They follow the survivor into the marriage bed, and they surface in ways that are confusing to both spouses. The trauma survivor may want intimacy desperately and find themselves unable to receive it. Their body freezes. Their mind disconnects. The very act that is supposed to express love triggers a cascade of fear responses that have nothing to do with their spouse and everything to do with what happened before their spouse existed.</p>

<p>For others, the withdrawal is medical. Antidepressants suppress desire with a reliability that no one warns patients about until it is too late. Hormonal changes after childbirth, during perimenopause, or as a side effect of other treatments can reduce desire to near zero. Chronic pain makes the physical act of sex difficult or impossible. These are not choices. They are conditions, and the church that treats them as spiritual failures — as a lack of willingness to give one's body to one's spouse — adds cruelty to suffering.</p>

<p>For still others, the withdrawal is relational. It is the accumulated residue of years of unresolved conflict, emotional distance, criticism, contempt, or neglect. The spouse who has been belittled at the dinner table cannot be expected to open themselves in the bedroom. The body keeps score. It registers every dismissive comment, every eye roll, every evening spent in separate rooms staring at separate screens. And eventually it says no — not as a punishment, but as a protection.</p>

<p>And for some, the withdrawal is simply the mystery of desire — that desire has its own logic, its own seasons, its own stubborn refusal to be summoned on command. The church that insists desire is a choice — that you can decide to want your spouse — is speaking a language that does not correspond to how human beings actually work. Desire is not a faucet. You cannot turn it on by trying harder.</p>

<h2>The Spouse Who Waits</h2>

<p>The spouse who desires intimacy and does not receive it inhabits a particular kind of loneliness. It is not the loneliness of being alone. It is the loneliness of being with someone — sleeping next to someone, sharing a life with someone — and feeling untouched. Unwanted. Invisible.</p>

<p>This spouse often carries shame about their desire. The culture sends contradictory messages: men are supposed to want sex all the time, which means that the man in a sexless marriage feels like a failure; women are not supposed to want sex as much as men, which means that the woman in a sexless marriage feels like a deviant. Both of these scripts are false, and both cause real damage.</p>

<p>The desiring spouse may begin to question their attractiveness, their worth, their identity. If my spouse does not want me, what does that say about me? This question, asked in enough silent moments, becomes a conviction: I am not enough. I am not desirable. Something is wrong with me. And the longer the sexlessness continues, the deeper the conviction roots.</p>

<p>The church must speak to this spouse without patronizing them. You are not wrong to want physical intimacy with the person you married. Your desire is not excessive or sinful or needy. It is the desire that the covenant itself creates — the longing for the union that the vows promised. The absence of that union is a real loss, and you are allowed to grieve it.</p>

<h2>The Spouse Who Cannot</h2>

<p>The spouse who withholds intimacy — and I use that word carefully, because "withhold" implies agency, and many of these spouses do not experience themselves as withholding anything — carries their own burden. They know what their spouse wants. They know they are not providing it. And they feel, most days, like a failure — or worse, like a fraud. They stood at the altar and promised themselves to this person, body and soul, and their body is not keeping the promise their mouth made.</p>

<p>The guilt is corrosive. It manifests as defensiveness — "Why is sex the only thing that matters to you?" — or as avoidance — staying up late, going to bed early, engineering the schedule so that opportunities for intimacy never arise. These are not strategies of cruelty. They are strategies of shame. The spouse who avoids intimacy is often avoiding the reminder of their own inadequacy.</p>

<p>The church must speak to this spouse with equal honesty and equal compassion. You are not broken. You are not failing at marriage because your body or your desire does not cooperate with your intentions. But you owe your spouse the truth about what you are experiencing — not performance, not excuses, but the vulnerable, frightening truth. "I do not know why I cannot want this. I am sorry. I want to find out." That sentence, spoken honestly, is more intimate than any physical act.</p>

<h2>The Path Forward</h2>

<p>There is no formula for restoring sexual intimacy to a marriage that has lost it. Anyone who offers you five steps is selling something. But there are practices — honest, difficult, slow practices — that create the conditions in which intimacy can grow.</p>

<p>The first is naming. Say what is happening. Out loud. To each other. "We have not been intimate in four months, and I need to talk about why." This sentence is terrifying. It is also the door through which every other conversation must pass. The sexless marriage that remains sexless usually does so because both spouses have agreed, implicitly, never to name what is happening. Breaking that agreement is the first act of intimacy — the intimacy of truth.</p>

<p>The second is curiosity. Not accusation — curiosity. "What happens for you when I initiate?" "What would feel safe?" "What are you afraid of?" These questions require the desiring spouse to set aside their hurt long enough to listen. They require the reluctant spouse to set aside their shame long enough to answer. Both of these are acts of tremendous courage.</p>

<p>The third is professional help. A good therapist — specifically, a therapist trained in sexual health, not just general marital counseling — can name what the couple cannot name. They can identify patterns, address trauma, explore medical factors, and provide a framework for the conversation that neither spouse can provide on their own. The church that refuses to recommend therapy because it believes prayer should be sufficient is not protecting the sanctity of marriage. It is abandoning couples to a problem they cannot solve alone.</p>

<p>The fourth is patience. Sexual intimacy, once lost, does not return quickly. The couple that expects resolution in weeks will be disappointed. The recovery is measured in months, sometimes years — in small gestures, tentative approaches, setbacks that feel like failures but are actually part of the process. The couple needs to know that the process itself is the faithfulness. The willingness to keep showing up, to keep having the hard conversation, to keep reaching across the distance — that willingness is the covenant in action.</p>

<h2>What the Church Must Say</h2>

<p>The church must say what it has been afraid to say: that the absence of sexual intimacy in marriage is a serious matter that deserves serious pastoral attention. Not a joke. Not a punchline. Not a footnote in a sermon about something else. A serious matter.</p>

<p>The church must stop treating sex as a reward for spiritual maturity and start treating it as a dimension of the relationship that, like every other dimension, is subject to brokenness, requires grace, and demands honest engagement. The couple in the sexless marriage is not less spiritual than the couple with a flourishing sex life. They are carrying a wound that the church should help them tend, not a sin the church should help them confess.</p>

<p>And the church must make room for the grief. The spouse who has not been touched in years is grieving something real — the loss of a dimension of marriage that they were told would be a gift and that has become an absence. The church that cannot sit with that grief — that rushes to fix it or minimize it or spiritualize it — is not serving these marriages. It is abandoning them.</p>

<p>The sexless marriage is a crisis of intimacy, and the church that claims to know something about intimacy — with God, with neighbor, with spouse — must find the courage to enter this crisis with honesty, compassion, and the willingness to stay for as long as the healing takes.</p>

<p>Which is usually longer than anyone wants it to be.</p>`
