    body: `<p>I need to say something the church does not want to hear, and I need to say it without qualification or hedge: there are marriages in which divorce is not the failure of covenant but the recognition that the covenant has already been destroyed — by violence, by sustained cruelty, by a pattern of behavior so destructive that remaining in the marriage requires the annihilation of the person God made you to be.</p>

<p>I say this as a pastor. I say this as someone who has performed weddings and meant every word of the liturgy. I say this as someone who believes marriage is a covenant, not a contract, and that covenants are meant to be kept. But I also say it as someone who has sat across from people — mostly women, though not exclusively — who stayed in marriages that were destroying them because the church told them that God required it. Who endured abuse because their pastor said suffering was redemptive. Who watched their children absorb patterns of cruelty because leaving was a sin and staying was faithfulness.</p>

<p>That theology is wrong. It is not just pastorally insensitive. It is theologically wrong. And the damage it has done — the lives it has diminished, the children it has harmed, the image of God it has defaced in the name of preserving the image of God — demands a reckoning that the church has been too comfortable to undertake.</p>

<h2>What Scripture Actually Says</h2>

<p>The church's case against divorce rests primarily on two texts: Malachi 2:16, traditionally translated "I hate divorce, says the Lord," and Jesus's statement in Matthew 19:6, "What God has joined together, let no one separate." Both texts are real. Both are authoritative. And both have been read with a selectivity that borders on dishonesty.</p>

<p>The Malachi passage, in its fuller context, is not a blanket prohibition against divorce. It is a prophetic indictment of men who were divorcing their wives to marry younger women — a specific cultural practice in which powerful men discarded faithful wives. The prophet is not addressing the woman fleeing an abusive husband. He is addressing the husband who treats his wife as disposable. To read this passage as a command that the discarded wife must remain discarded is to invert the text's own moral logic.</p>

<p>Jesus's statement in Matthew 19 is similarly contextual. He is responding to Pharisees who ask whether a man can divorce his wife "for any cause" — a reference to the debate between the schools of Hillel and Shammai over the interpretation of Deuteronomy 24:1. The school of Hillel permitted divorce for almost any reason, including burning dinner. Jesus sides against this permissiveness, insisting that marriage is a covenant, not a convenience. But in the same passage, he names an exception: porneia — sexual immorality. And in Matthew 5:32, he uses the same exception, indicating that this was a consistent element of his teaching, not a one-time concession.</p>

<p>Paul adds another exception in 1 Corinthians 7:15: if an unbelieving spouse departs, the believing spouse "is not bound." The word Paul uses — dedoulotai — is a slavery term. The believing spouse is not enslaved to the marriage. This is strong language, stronger than the church typically acknowledges.</p>

<p>The point is not that Scripture takes divorce lightly. It does not. The point is that Scripture's treatment of divorce is more nuanced, more contextual, and more compassionate than the church's treatment of divorce. The Bible knows what the church often refuses to know: that some marriages should not continue. That the preservation of the institution is not always the preservation of the people within it. That a God who liberates slaves and defends the oppressed does not require the oppressed to remain in bondage to a covenant that has become a weapon.</p>

<h2>The Covenant That Was Already Broken</h2>

<p>The deepest error in the church's theology of divorce is the assumption that the person who files for divorce is the person who broke the covenant. In many cases, the covenant was broken long before the paperwork was filed. It was broken by the first act of violence. It was broken by the sustained pattern of contempt. It was broken by the addiction that the addicted spouse refused to address. It was broken by the infidelity that the unfaithful spouse refused to end.</p>

<p>Divorce, in these cases, is not the breaking of the covenant. It is the legal recognition that the covenant has already been broken — by someone else. The spouse who files for divorce after years of abuse is not violating their vows. They are acknowledging that their vows were violated — by the person who promised to love, honor, and cherish and instead chose to harm, demean, and destroy.</p>

<p>This distinction matters enormously, and the church's failure to make it has caused incalculable suffering. The abused spouse who stays because "I made a vow" is keeping a vow to a covenant that no longer exists. The other party destroyed it. What remains is not a covenant. It is a cage dressed in the language of covenant, and no theology worth the name should require anyone to remain in it.</p>

<h2>The Children Question</h2>

<p>The church's strongest argument against divorce — the one that gives even the most progressive pastors pause — is the children. "Stay together for the kids." The research is cited, usually selectively: children of divorce have worse outcomes on a range of measures. This is true, as far as it goes. But the research the church does not cite is equally clear: children raised in high-conflict marriages have outcomes that are as bad or worse than children of divorce. The child who watches one parent belittle, control, or strike the other is not being protected by the preservation of the marriage. They are being educated in what marriage looks like, and the lesson they are learning will shape every relationship they have for the rest of their lives.</p>

<p>The question is not whether children suffer when their parents divorce. They do. The question is whether they suffer more from the divorce or from the marriage — and in cases of abuse, addiction, or sustained high conflict, the answer is clear. The most loving thing a parent can do for their child is not to preserve the marriage at all costs. It is to show the child what health looks like, what dignity looks like, what it means to refuse to accept destruction as normal.</p>

<p>I have watched children bloom after a divorce — children who had been anxious, withdrawn, hypervigilant, who began to relax when the household that had been a war zone became two households that were peaceful. I do not celebrate this. I grieve that it was necessary. But I refuse to pretend that the marriage was better for them than the divorce, because I watched what the marriage was doing to them, and it was not good.</p>

<h2>The Stigma the Church Creates</h2>

<p>The divorced person in the church occupies a particular kind of exile. They are present but marked. Their status is known. In some congregations, they are explicitly barred from leadership, from teaching, from serving communion. In others, the exclusion is subtler — a certain distance in conversation, an absence of invitations, the sense that they have been reclassified from full member to cautionary tale.</p>

<p>This stigma is not biblical. It is cultural. It is the church's anxiety about its own failure to prevent the divorce, projected onto the person who had the courage to end a destructive marriage. And it is devastating to people who are already carrying the weight of a life they did not plan — who are already grieving, already questioning, already wondering whether God has abandoned them the way the church seems to have.</p>

<p>Jesus was remarkably gentle with divorced people. His conversation with the Samaritan woman at the well — a woman who had been married five times and was now living with a man who was not her husband — contains not a single word of condemnation. He sees her. He knows her. He offers her living water. The church that claims to follow Jesus should follow him here: into the conversation with the divorced person that begins not with judgment but with presence.</p>

<h2>When Staying Is the Unfaithful Option</h2>

<p>I want to push further than the church typically allows. I want to say not just that divorce is sometimes permissible but that staying is sometimes wrong. Not just inadvisable. Wrong.</p>

<p>When a spouse is being physically abused, staying puts them and their children at risk of serious harm or death. Staying is not faithfulness. It is complicity in one's own destruction, and God does not require it.</p>

<p>When a spouse is being systematically degraded — told they are worthless, stupid, ugly, incompetent — staying cooperates with the destruction of the image of God in a human person. The imago Dei is not merely a theological concept. It is the most fundamental truth about every human being, and the marriage that systematically assaults it is not a covenant worth preserving.</p>

<p>When a spouse is addicted and refuses treatment, staying enables the addiction. The language of enabling is not therapeutic jargon. It is a description of a real dynamic: the spouse who stays, who covers, who compensates, who absorbs the consequences — that spouse is making it possible for the addiction to continue. Sometimes the most loving thing you can do for an addict is to leave, because leaving is the only thing that might make the cost of the addiction high enough to motivate change.</p>

<p>When a spouse is unfaithful and unrepentant — not a single transgression met with genuine remorse, but a pattern of infidelity accompanied by blame-shifting, gaslighting, and refusal to change — staying teaches the unfaithful spouse that their behavior has no consequences. Forgiveness without repentance is not grace. It is permission.</p>

<p>In each of these cases, staying is not the faithful option. It is the option that protects the institution at the expense of the person. And no institution — not even marriage — is more valuable than the person God made.</p>

<h2>The Grace on the Other Side</h2>

<p>I have walked with people through divorce. I have sat with them on the night they signed the papers. I have listened to them grieve the life they thought they would have and did not get. And I have watched many of them — not all, but many — emerge on the other side into a life that is quieter, healthier, and more honest than the one they left.</p>

<p>This does not mean the divorce was easy. It was not. It does not mean the divorce was painless. It was excruciating. But it means that the God who promises to make all things new does not limit that promise to people who stayed married. The divorced person is not damaged goods. They are a person who survived something, who made a terrible and necessary choice, and who is now standing in a place they never planned to stand, looking for the grace that the church has told them they forfeited.</p>

<p>They did not forfeit it. Grace is not forfeited by divorce any more than it is forfeited by any other human brokenness. The entire point of grace — the entire scandal of grace — is that it goes to the places where human effort has failed and human plans have collapsed and human covenants have been broken. If grace does not go there, it does not go anywhere that matters.</p>

<p>The church must learn to say this. Not mumble it. Not whisper it in private conversations while maintaining the public theology that divorce is always a failure. Say it: sometimes divorce is the faithful option. Sometimes it is the only faithful option. And the God who hates divorce also hates the conditions that make divorce necessary, and the person who chose divorce over destruction chose well.</p>

<p>They chose life. And the God who sets before us life and death and says "choose life" — that God is not ashamed of them.</p>

<p>Even if the church is.</p>`
