    body: `<p>There was a time when they talked. Not about the children's schedules or the broken dishwasher or who was picking up the prescription. They talked about ideas, about memories, about the article one of them read that morning. They talked about nothing in particular for hours, the way you do with someone whose company you enjoy — not because the conversation was important, but because the person was.</p>

<p>They cannot remember when that stopped. It was not a moment. It was an erosion — so gradual that neither of them noticed until the evening came when they sat across from each other at dinner and realized they had nothing to say. Not nothing worth saying. Nothing. The well was dry. The friendship that had been the foundation of the marriage — the thing that made them choose each other in the first place — had been replaced by a management partnership. They were efficient together. They ran the household. They divided the labor. They were, by every functional measure, a successful team. And they were lonely in a way that only people who share a life with someone they no longer know can be lonely.</p>

<p>This is the marriage that lost its friendship. It is not dramatic. There is no affair, no addiction, no crisis that would justify a counselor's appointment or a pastor's intervention. It is simply two people who like each other less than they used to — or, more precisely, two people who have forgotten how to like each other, because liking requires attention, and attention has been directed elsewhere for so long that neither of them knows how to redirect it.</p>

<h2>How Friendship Dies in Marriage</h2>

<p>The friendship in a marriage dies by neglect, and the neglect has identifiable stages. The first stage is the prioritization of function over connection. This begins, usually, with the arrival of children. The couple that used to spend Saturday mornings in bed talking now spends Saturday mornings at soccer games and grocery stores. The couple that used to cook together now eats in shifts. The couple that used to read aloud to each other now reads separately, in different rooms, after the children are in bed — if they read at all, if they have the energy, if they are not already asleep.</p>

<p>This is understandable. Children require attention, and the attention must come from somewhere. But what the couple does not realize, in those early years of parenting, is that they are making a withdrawal from an account they are not replenishing. The friendship was built by hours of shared attention. It is being starved by the redirection of that attention to the demands of the household. And when the demands eventually subside — when the children are older, when the schedule loosens — the couple discovers that the friendship they assumed would be waiting for them has died in their absence.</p>

<p>The second stage is the replacement of curiosity with assumption. In the early years, you asked your spouse questions because you did not know the answers. What do you think about this? How do you feel about that? What would you do if? Over time, you stop asking because you think you already know. You assume you know what your spouse thinks about politics, about faith, about the neighbor's new car. And in most cases, you are right — you do know. But the act of asking was never primarily about acquiring information. It was about expressing interest. It was the verbal equivalent of turning toward someone and saying, with your attention, "You matter to me. Your inner life is worth my curiosity." When the asking stops, the message that replaces it is: I already know everything about you. There is nothing new to discover. You are a solved equation.</p>

<p>Nobody wants to be a solved equation.</p>

<p>The third stage is the accumulation of unprocessed grievances. Every marriage generates friction, and healthy marriages process the friction through conversation — through the small, ongoing negotiations that keep two people calibrated. But the marriage that has lost its friendship has also lost the channel through which friction is processed. The conversation that should happen — "It bothered me when you said that" — does not happen, because the couple no longer has the relational credit to absorb the vulnerability that honest conversation requires. Instead, the grievances accumulate. They pile up like snow on a roof, invisible from the outside, adding weight that the structure was not designed to carry.</p>

<p>Eventually, the roof collapses — not under the weight of any single grievance, but under the accumulated weight of years of unspoken ones. And both spouses are bewildered, because none of the individual grievances seem heavy enough to explain the collapse. They are not heavy enough. Individually. Together, they are crushing.</p>

<h2>What John Gottman Found</h2>

<p>The research of John Gottman — the psychologist who has studied marriage with more rigor and over a longer period than anyone else in the field — consistently identifies friendship as the most important predictor of marital satisfaction and stability. Not passion. Not compatibility. Not shared values or shared interests or shared faith. Friendship. The simple, unspectacular reality of two people who know each other, like each other, and turn toward each other when the world turns against them.</p>

<p>Gottman's concept of "bids for connection" is particularly illuminating. A bid is any attempt by one spouse to connect with the other — a comment, a question, a touch, a look. "Look at that bird" is a bid. "How was your meeting?" is a bid. Reaching for your spouse's hand while watching television is a bid. Gottman found that couples who stayed together turned toward their partner's bids 86 percent of the time. Couples who divorced turned toward only 33 percent of the time.</p>

<p>The numbers are stark, but the mechanism is intuitive. Every bid is a test — a small, often unconscious test of whether the other person is available, interested, present. When the bid is met — when the spouse looks at the bird, answers the question, takes the offered hand — the message is: I am here. You are not alone. We are connected. When the bid is ignored — when the spouse does not look up, does not answer, does not take the hand — the message is: you do not matter enough to interrupt what I am doing. And enough of those messages, received over enough years, will kill the friendship as surely as oxygen deprivation kills a flame.</p>

<h2>What the Church Can Say</h2>

<p>The church has a theology of marriage as covenant, as sacrament, as a reflection of Christ and the church. What it lacks is a theology of marriage as friendship — a framework that takes seriously the daily, unglamorous, absolutely essential practice of liking the person you love.</p>

<p>The Song of Solomon provides the raw material. "This is my beloved and this is my friend," the woman declares. The two categories are not separated. They are held together, and the holding together is the point. The beloved is the friend. The friend is the beloved. The marriage that loses the friendship has not merely lost a convenience. It has lost a constitutive element — the element without which the covenant, however intact, is hollow.</p>

<p>The church should say this: friendship in marriage is not optional. It is not a bonus that some couples enjoy and others manage without. It is the soil in which everything else — sex, parenting, conflict resolution, spiritual growth — takes root. Without it, the marriage can function, but it cannot flourish. And God did not institute marriage so that two people could function together. God said, "It is not good for the man to be alone," and the solution was not a business partner or a roommate. It was a companion. A friend.</p>

<h2>The Recovery of Friendship</h2>

<p>Friendship in marriage can be recovered, but the recovery requires intentionality that feels, at first, artificial. The couple that has not had a real conversation in months cannot simply decide to start having real conversations. They have to relearn the skill — and it is a skill — of being curious about each other, of turning toward bids, of carving out time that is not functional but relational.</p>

<p>This means dates. Not the performative date night that the church has elevated to a sacrament — not the expensive dinner where both people sit across from each other trying to remember how to talk. Simple dates. A walk. A drive. An errand done together instead of divided for efficiency. The goal is not romance. The goal is proximity without agenda — the condition in which friendship naturally grows.</p>

<p>It means questions. Real questions, not the questions you already know the answers to. "What are you worried about that you have not told me?" "What is the best part of your life right now?" "If you could change one thing about our daily routine, what would it be?" These questions require vulnerability from the asker and the answerer, and vulnerability is the currency of friendship.</p>

<p>It means turning off the screens. The phone, the laptop, the television — these are the enemies of friendship, not because technology is evil but because technology provides an endless supply of stimulation that requires nothing from you. Friendship requires something from you. It requires your attention, your presence, your willingness to be bored together — because boredom, shared, is the precondition for the spontaneous conversation, the unexpected discovery, the moment of connection that no algorithm can engineer.</p>

<h2>What Friendship Makes Possible</h2>

<p>The marriage that recovers its friendship does not merely become happier. It becomes more resilient. The friendship is the shock absorber — the thing that cushions the marriage against the inevitable impacts of life in a fallen world. The couple that likes each other can survive a financial crisis. The couple that has lost its friendship may not survive a bad week.</p>

<p>Friendship also makes the hard conversations possible. The couple that is friends can say, "I need to tell you something difficult" without the other person bracing for catastrophe. The friendship creates a context of safety — a confidence that the person across from you is for you, not against you, and that the difficult thing they are about to say comes from a place of care rather than contempt.</p>

<p>And friendship makes the marriage worth fighting for. The couple in crisis who can still say, "I like this person — I have forgotten how to show it, but I like who they are" — that couple has something to build on. The couple who cannot say it, who have not liked each other in years, who stay together out of obligation or fear or inertia — that couple has a harder road, though not an impossible one.</p>

<p>The recovery of friendship in marriage is not the recovery of the past. You cannot go back to the people you were when the friendship was new. You can go forward to the people you are now — older, more tired, more complicated, and more in need of a friend than you have ever been.</p>

<p>The friend is there. Across the table. Across the silence. Waiting to be rediscovered.</p>

<p>All it takes is turning toward them. And saying something. Anything. Even: "Look at that bird."</p>`
