    body: `<p>No passage of Scripture has been more weaponized in the history of marriage than Ephesians 5:22-33. "Wives, submit to your husbands as to the Lord." Those eleven words have been quoted to justify everything from benign patriarchy to outright abuse, from the gentle insistence that the husband should make the final decision to the violent demand that the wife should obey without question. They have been embroidered on pillows, preached from pulpits, and whispered by abusers to their victims as theological justification for cruelty.</p>

<p>And they have been quoted, almost always, out of context. Torn from the passage that surrounds them, stripped of the revolutionary framework in which Paul placed them, and deployed as a proof text for a power arrangement that the passage itself subverts.</p>

<p>It is time to read the text again. Not the eleven words. The whole passage. And not with the assumptions that two thousand years of patriarchal interpretation have layered onto it, but with the eyes of someone encountering Paul's argument for the first time, in the context in which it was written, to the audience that first received it.</p>

<p>What we will find is not what either the complementarians or the egalitarians expect. What we will find is harder, more beautiful, and more costly than either camp has been willing to say.</p>

<h2>The Verse They Always Skip</h2>

<p>The passage does not begin at verse 22. It begins at verse 21: "Submit to one another out of reverence for Christ." This verse is the heading — the thesis statement — of everything that follows. Paul's instruction to wives does not emerge from a vacuum. It emerges from a prior, overarching command to mutual submission. Every relationship that Paul addresses in the following verses — wives and husbands, children and parents, slaves and masters — is nested under this controlling statement: submit to one another.</p>

<p>This matters enormously. If verse 22 is read in isolation, it establishes a one-directional hierarchy: wives submit to husbands. But if verse 22 is read as a specific application of verse 21, the hierarchy is reframed. The wife's submission is one expression of a mutual submission that characterizes the entire Christian community. The husband is not exempted from submission. He is included in it by the verse that governs the passage.</p>

<p>The church has, for most of its history, read verse 22 without verse 21. This is not an accident. It is a choice — a choice made by interpreters who benefited from the hierarchical reading and who had every incentive to ignore the verse that complicated it. When you begin at verse 22, you get patriarchy. When you begin at verse 21, you get something else entirely. You get a community in which power is not exercised over others but surrendered for others. You get a marriage in which both spouses are called to the same fundamental posture: submission — not to each other's authority, but to each other's good.</p>

<h2>What "Submit" Actually Means</h2>

<p>The Greek word translated "submit" is hupotasso. In its military usage, it means to arrange oneself under — to place oneself in a position of subordination within an ordered structure. But Paul is not writing a military manual. He is writing to a community that has been turned upside down by the gospel, in which the first are last and the last are first, in which the greatest is the servant of all. In this context, hupotasso does not mean "obey a superior." It means "orient yourself toward the good of another person, even at cost to yourself."</p>

<p>This is a crucial distinction. Obedience is compelled by authority. Submission, in Paul's usage, is offered by love. It is not the response of an inferior to a superior. It is the response of a free person who chooses, voluntarily, to prioritize another's well-being. It is what Jesus modeled in the foot-washing — the one with all the authority choosing to serve the one with none. The submission Paul calls for is not the submission of weakness. It is the submission of strength voluntarily restrained for the sake of love.</p>

<p>The wife who submits in this sense is not diminishing herself. She is orienting herself — toward her husband's good, toward the good of the marriage, toward the pattern of Christ who submitted himself to death for the sake of those he loved. This is not a demeaning posture. It is the most exalted posture the New Testament knows.</p>

<p>But — and this is where the complementarian reading goes wrong — it is not a posture required only of wives.</p>

<h2>What the Husband Is Actually Told</h2>

<p>Paul's instruction to husbands occupies three times the space of his instruction to wives. This is not an accident. In a first-century context, the wife's submission was assumed. It was the cultural default. The husband's authority was unquestioned. Paul did not need to spend time establishing what everyone already accepted. What he needed to establish — what required argument, what was genuinely radical — was the limitation and redefinition of that authority.</p>

<p>"Husbands, love your wives as Christ loved the church and gave himself up for her." This is not a general instruction to be nice. It is a specific instruction to die. The model Paul holds up is not Christ the King, not Christ the Ruler, not Christ the Head who exercises power over the body. It is Christ the Crucified — the one who emptied himself, took the form of a servant, and was obedient unto death. The husband's love is to look like that.</p>

<p>In a first-century household — in a world where the husband had near-absolute legal authority over his wife, where he could divorce her at will, where he owned her property and controlled her social access — this instruction was not a reinforcement of the status quo. It was its demolition. Paul tells the husband: your authority is real, and it exists for one purpose only — to be spent on behalf of your wife. You do not exercise it over her. You exercise it for her. You do not use your position to get what you want. You use your position to give what she needs. And if the giving costs you everything — if it costs you your comfort, your preferences, your very life — that is precisely the cost Christ paid, and it is the cost you signed up for when you entered this covenant.</p>

<p>The husband who reads Ephesians 5 and concludes that he is in charge has read three verses and missed the other nine. He has seen his authority and missed its purpose. He has claimed the throne and ignored the cross that stands behind it.</p>

<h2>The Head-Body Metaphor</h2>

<p>Paul says the husband is the "head" of the wife as Christ is the head of the church. The word "head" — kephale in Greek — has generated more scholarly debate than perhaps any other word in the Pauline corpus. Does it mean "authority over," as the complementarian tradition insists? Or does it mean "source," as some egalitarian scholars have argued?</p>

<p>The honest answer is that the debate is not settled, and both sides have legitimate arguments. But what is more important than the precise meaning of kephale is the model Paul attaches to it. Whatever "head" means, it means what Christ's headship means. And Christ's headship, as Paul describes it in this passage, means giving himself up — not exercising authority over the church, but sacrificing himself for the church's good.</p>

<p>If "head" means "authority," then the authority is self-sacrificial. If "head" means "source," then the source is self-giving. Either way, the husband's role is defined not by what he gets from the relationship but by what he gives to it. The head that does not serve the body is not a head in any Christian sense. It is a tumor.</p>

<p>This is not what the complementarian tradition has typically taught. The typical complementarian teaching says: the husband leads, the wife follows. The husband decides, the wife submits. The husband is the head, the wife is the body. These formulations, while they sound like Ephesians 5, actually reverse the passage's logic. The passage does not say the husband leads and the wife follows. It says both submit — the wife through willing orientation toward her husband's good, the husband through self-sacrificial love that takes Christ's death as its model. Both postures are forms of submission. Neither is a claim to power.</p>

<h2>What the Passage Does Not Say</h2>

<p>Ephesians 5 does not say the husband makes the decisions. It does not say the wife must obey. It does not say the husband has the final word. It does not say the wife cannot lead, teach, earn, think, or speak. It does not say the husband's opinion carries more weight. It does not establish a chain of command. It does not create a hierarchy of value, worth, intelligence, or spiritual capacity.</p>

<p>These are things the church has read into the passage — projecting the patriarchal assumptions of its culture onto a text that was, in its original context, far more subversive than the church has been willing to recognize. First-century wives did not need to be told to submit; the culture already required it. What was revolutionary was the instruction to husbands — the limitation of their authority, the redefinition of their headship, the insistence that their power existed solely for the purpose of self-sacrificial love.</p>

<p>The tragedy of the church's use of Ephesians 5 is that it has taken a passage that originally limited the husband's power and used it to expand it. It has taken a passage that originally elevated the wife's dignity and used it to diminish it. It has turned a revolutionary text into a reactionary one — and in doing so, it has done violence to the text, to women, and to the marriages that the text was meant to serve.</p>

<h2>What Mutual Submission Looks Like</h2>

<p>If Ephesians 5 is read as Paul intended — beginning at verse 21, with mutual submission as the controlling principle — what does it produce? Not the complementarian hierarchy. Not the egalitarian erasure of all difference. Something harder and more beautiful than either.</p>

<p>It produces a marriage in which both spouses are constantly asking: what does love require me to give up? Not what does love entitle me to. What does love require me to surrender? This is the question that the cross asks every relationship, and it is the question that mutual submission embeds in the daily life of the marriage.</p>

<p>Sometimes love requires the wife to defer to her husband — not because he has authority over her, but because his judgment is sound and her deference serves the good of the family. Sometimes love requires the husband to defer to his wife — not because she has authority over him, but because her wisdom exceeds his and his deference serves the good of the family. The direction of deference is not fixed by gender. It is determined by wisdom, by context, by the specific needs of the specific moment. And both spouses are willing to defer, because both spouses have internalized the same principle: I am here to serve, not to be served.</p>

<p>This is harder than hierarchy. Hierarchy is simple: someone is in charge, someone is not. Mutual submission is complex: both are in charge, both are not, and the negotiation of who leads in which moment requires a level of maturity, trust, and communication that hierarchy does not demand. But the difficulty is the point. Marriage is supposed to be a crucible — a context in which both people are shaped into the image of Christ. And the shape of Christ is not the shape of authority. It is the shape of a cross.</p>

<h2>The Cost of Getting This Wrong</h2>

<p>The church's misreading of Ephesians 5 has had consequences that cannot be measured. Women who were told that God required their submission to men who were harming them. Men who were told that their authority was divinely sanctioned and therefore beyond question. Marriages in which one person's voice was elevated and the other's was silenced — not by mutual agreement, but by a theological framework that was presented as biblical and was, in fact, a cultural projection onto a text that said something else entirely.</p>

<p>I have sat with the women who were damaged by this misreading. I have heard their stories. I have watched them struggle to disentangle the God they were taught from the God who actually exists — the God who does not require their diminishment, who does not sanction their suffering, who does not use the language of love to enforce the reality of control.</p>

<p>And I have sat with the men who were damaged by it too — men who were told they were the leaders and discovered, to their bewilderment, that the crown was heavy and the authority was isolating and the marriage they were supposed to lead was dying because their wife did not need a leader. She needed a partner. She needed a friend. She needed someone who would submit to her — to her needs, her wisdom, her experience, her voice — the way she had been told, all her life, to submit to him.</p>

<p>Ephesians 5 is one of the most beautiful passages in Scripture. It envisions a marriage in which both people pour themselves out for each other, in which authority is exercised as service, in which the pattern of Christ's self-giving love becomes the pattern of daily life. The church has turned it into a hierarchy. It is time to turn it back.</p>

<p>The cross is not a throne. And the marriage that is built on the cross is not built on power. It is built on the willingness to lay power down.</p>`
