    body: `<p>Five years is not a milestone the culture celebrates. There are no special names for a fifth anniversary — no silver, no gold, no diamond. Hallmark makes a card for it, but the card does not know what to say, because five years is an awkward interval: too long to be new, too short to be tested, too ordinary to merit the kind of reflection that longer marriages receive.</p>

<p>But five years is exactly when the reflection is most needed. Because five years is when the marriage you actually have begins to diverge from the marriage you imagined, and the gap between the two — the gap between what you thought this would be and what it is — is either a source of grief or a source of growth, depending on whether you have the courage to look at it honestly.</p>

<p>I have performed enough weddings and counseled enough couples to know that the first two years are consumed by adjustment — by the shock of sharing a bathroom, a budget, a bed with another person whose habits were formed in a different household. Years three and four are consumed by whatever comes next — often a child, sometimes a career change, occasionally a crisis that forces the couple to discover what they are made of before they were ready to find out. But year five — year five is when the dust settles. The patterns are established. The routines are set. The way you fight, the way you make up, the way you spend Saturday mornings — all of it has calcified into a shape. And the question year five asks is: is this a shape you can live in?</p>

<h2>The Questions Nobody Asks</h2>

<p>The five-year marriage check begins with questions, and the questions must be honest enough to produce honest answers. Not "Are you happy?" — happiness is too vague, too subject to the weather of any given Tuesday, to be a useful diagnostic. The questions must be more specific, more pointed, more willing to cut through the comfortable fictions that five years of shared life inevitably generate.</p>

<p>Do you like this person? Not love — like. Love is a commitment. Like is a preference. You can love someone you do not like, and many five-year marriages are doing exactly that — maintaining the commitment while losing the preference. Do you enjoy their company? Do you look forward to seeing them at the end of the day? Would you choose them again — not in the romantic, cinematic sense, but in the practical sense: if you met them today, knowing what you know, would you want to build a life with this person?</p>

<p>Do you trust this person? Not with the big things — most people trust their spouse not to clean out the bank account or disappear with the children. With the small things. Do you trust them with your feelings? With your failures? With the parts of yourself that you do not show anyone else? Is this person safe? Not safe in the sense of boring. Safe in the sense of: I can be my full self here, including the parts that are weak and foolish and afraid, and I will not be punished for it.</p>

<p>Do you fight well? Not "do you fight" — every couple fights, and the couple that does not fight is a couple that has stopped caring enough to disagree. The question is whether the fighting produces resolution or resentment. Do your arguments end with understanding, even if they do not end with agreement? Or do they end with someone winning and someone losing, with the loser filing the loss away in the growing archive of grievances that will eventually become too heavy to carry?</p>

<p>Are you growing — individually and together? The person you married five years ago is not the person you are married to now. People change. The question is whether you are changing in directions that bring you closer together or further apart. Are your interests diverging? Your values? Your vision of what a good life looks like? Some divergence is natural and healthy. But if the divergence is so great that you cannot find common ground — if you are becoming strangers who share an address — the five-year mark is the time to name it.</p>

<h2>The Patterns That Have Set</h2>

<p>By year five, the marriage has developed patterns that feel permanent — and that feel permanent because, without intervention, they are. The patterns of communication, conflict, intimacy, and daily life that characterize a five-year marriage are, with minor variations, the patterns that will characterize a fifteen-year marriage and a thirty-year marriage. The couple that does not fight fair at five will not fight fair at twenty. The couple that avoids hard conversations at five will avoid them at fifteen. The couple that has stopped having sex at five will not spontaneously resume at ten.</p>

<p>This is not fatalism. It is observation, supported by decades of marital research. Patterns, once established, have inertia. They persist not because the couple chooses them but because changing them requires more energy than maintaining them. The couple that wants to change a destructive pattern at year five has time and plasticity on their side. The couple that waits until year fifteen has less of both.</p>

<p>The five-year check is, therefore, an opportunity — perhaps the best opportunity the marriage will ever have — to identify the patterns that are not working and to change them before they harden into permanence. This requires honesty that most couples find uncomfortable. It requires saying, "This thing we do — this way we fight, this way we avoid, this way we coexist without connecting — this is not working, and if we do not change it, it will define us."</p>

<p>That sentence is hard to speak. But it is infinitely easier to speak at year five than at year twenty, when the pattern has been reinforced by fifteen additional years of repetition and the prospect of change feels not just difficult but impossible.</p>

<h2>The Myth of Effortless Marriage</h2>

<p>The five-year mark is often the point at which the couple confronts one of the church's most damaging myths: that a good marriage is an effortless marriage. The myth says: if you married the right person, and if you are both walking with God, the marriage should be natural, easy, a source of joy rather than work. And when the marriage is not easy — when it requires effort, negotiation, compromise, and the daily decision to choose patience over frustration — the myth whispers: something is wrong. You married the wrong person. God is not in this marriage. A good marriage would not be this hard.</p>

<p>This is a lie. Every good marriage is hard. It is hard because it requires two selfish people — and all people are selfish, at bottom, no matter how sanctified — to share a life in which their selfishness is constantly confronted, constantly challenged, constantly required to yield to the needs and desires of another person. This is not a sign that the marriage is failing. It is a sign that the marriage is doing what marriage is supposed to do: sanctifying the people in it. And sanctification is never comfortable.</p>

<p>The couple that reaches year five and finds their marriage difficult has not failed. They have arrived at the real marriage — the one that exists after the honeymoon fades and the infatuation wears off and the person across the table is revealed as a human being with morning breath and bad habits and a capacity for annoyance that the dating period mercifully concealed.</p>

<p>The real marriage is better than the imagined one. Not easier. Better. Because the real marriage is the place where actual love — not the feeling, but the practice — happens. The feeling of love is a gift. The practice of love is a discipline. And the five-year marriage is the place where the discipline either takes root or is abandoned in favor of the fantasy that somewhere, with someone else, the feeling would have lasted.</p>

<h2>What to Do with What You Find</h2>

<p>The five-year check may reveal that the marriage is healthier than you realized — that the daily frustrations had obscured a foundation of genuine affection, trust, and shared purpose. If so, name it. Gratitude is not a feeling. It is a practice, and the marriage that practices gratitude is a marriage that reinforces its own health.</p>

<p>The check may reveal problems that are serious but addressable — communication patterns that need repair, intimacy issues that need attention, unresolved conflicts that have been avoided for years and need to be engaged. If so, address them. Now. Not next month, not next year, not when things settle down. The things will not settle down. There will always be a reason to postpone the hard conversation. Do not wait for the reason to run out. The reason will never run out. The marriage might.</p>

<p>The check may reveal that the marriage is in genuine trouble — that the foundation is weaker than either person knew, that the distance between them is greater than adjustment can bridge, that professional help is not optional but urgent. If so, get the help. Not the book. Not the podcast. Not the marriage retreat that costs five hundred dollars and produces a temporary emotional high that fades by Tuesday. A therapist. A skilled, trained, experienced therapist who will sit with you for months and help you rebuild what the marriage requires and that you cannot do alone.</p>

<p>And the check may reveal, in rare cases, that the marriage is not viable — that it was built on a foundation that cannot support what a marriage needs, and that the most honest thing both people can do is to name this truth and reckon with its consequences. This is the hardest outcome, and the church must not treat it as the only possible outcome of honest assessment. Most marriages at year five are not beyond repair. They are under-maintained. And under-maintained is a fixable condition.</p>

<h2>An Invitation, Not a Judgment</h2>

<p>The five-year marriage check is not a test you pass or fail. It is an invitation to look — honestly, without flinching, without the optimistic distortions that protect you from reality and prevent you from change — at what you have built together, and to decide, with open eyes, whether to continue building it.</p>

<p>The couples I admire most are not the couples with the easiest marriages. They are the couples who looked honestly at what they had, named what was working and what was not, and chose — deliberately, sacrificially, with full knowledge of the cost — to keep building. They chose the marriage. Not because it was perfect. Because it was theirs, and because they believed that what could be built on the existing foundation was worth the effort of building it.</p>

<p>Five years is not a milestone. It is a crossroads. And the couple standing at it has a choice: drift forward on inertia, hoping that time will fix what attention has not, or stop, look, assess, and choose the marriage again — this time with the honesty that the first time lacked, because the first time was a promise about the future and this time is a decision about the present.</p>

<p>The decision about the present is always harder. It is also the only one that matters.</p>`
