    body: `<p>The Song of Solomon is the embarrassment of the biblical canon. It sits between Ecclesiastes and Isaiah — between the philosopher's despair and the prophet's fire — and it talks about bodies. It talks about breasts and thighs and lips and the curve of a waist. It talks about desire with an explicitness that has made the church uncomfortable for two thousand years and that has produced, in response, one of the most extraordinary feats of interpretive avoidance in the history of religious thought.</p>

<p>For most of the church's history, the Song was read as an allegory. The woman was the church. The man was Christ. The desire was spiritual longing. The bodies were metaphors. This reading allowed the church to include the Song in the canon without actually reckoning with what it said — to keep the book without keeping its meaning. The allegorical interpretation is not entirely without merit; the prophetic tradition does use marital language to describe the relationship between God and Israel. But as the primary or exclusive reading of the Song, it is an evasion. It takes a poem about human desire and sanitizes it into a poem about divine love, which is a way of saying that human desire is too dangerous, too unruly, too bodily to be trusted with its own meaning.</p>

<p>The Song will not submit to this sanitization. Read it on its own terms — as a poem, as a celebration, as a piece of ancient literature that found its way into the canon not despite its subject matter but because of it — and what you find is a theology of desire that the church desperately needs and has spent centuries avoiding.</p>

<h2>What the Song Says About Bodies</h2>

<p>The Song speaks about the human body with a directness that is startling, particularly in a religious text. The man describes the woman's body from head to foot — or, in the famous wasf of chapter 4, from eyes to breasts — with a delight that is unambiguously physical. "Your breasts are like two fawns, twins of a gazelle, that feed among the lilies." This is not allegory. This is a man looking at a woman's body and finding it beautiful, and saying so, in a text that the community of faith preserved as sacred.</p>

<p>The woman is equally direct. "My beloved is radiant and ruddy, distinguished among ten thousand. His head is the finest gold; his locks are wavy, black as a raven." She describes his arms, his body, his legs. She is not being coy. She is not being metaphorical. She is looking at a man and desiring him, and she is not ashamed of the desiring.</p>

<p>This lack of shame is the Song's most radical contribution. In a religious tradition that has struggled, from Augustine onward, with the relationship between the body and the spirit — in a tradition that has often treated the body as the spirit's enemy, as the source of temptation, as the thing that must be disciplined, denied, and overcome — the Song stands as a protest. The body is not the enemy. The body is the beloved. Desire is not sin. Desire is the energy that draws two people together in the most intimate form of knowing that human beings can experience.</p>

<p>The Hebrew word for the sexual act is yada — to know. The same word used for the deepest form of relational knowledge. The same word used for the knowledge of God. The Song takes this equation seriously. To know another person's body is to know them — not completely, not exhaustively, but in a way that no other form of knowing can replicate. The body is not an obstacle to intimacy. It is the medium of intimacy. And the Song celebrates this without apology.</p>

<h2>The Woman's Voice</h2>

<p>The most striking feature of the Song, from a literary perspective, is the prominence of the woman's voice. She speaks more than the man. She initiates more than the man. She desires more than the man — or at least, she expresses her desire more freely, more urgently, more insistently. "Let him kiss me with the kisses of his mouth." The Song begins with the woman's desire, not the man's. She is not waiting to be pursued. She is pursuing.</p>

<p>This is remarkable in any ancient text, but particularly in a text from the patriarchal world of ancient Israel. Women in the Hebrew Bible are usually spoken about, not spoken to; acted upon, not acting. The Song reverses this. The woman is the subject, not the object. She has agency, voice, desire, and the freedom to express all three without censure. She goes out at night looking for her beloved. She takes him to her mother's house. She is not passive. She is not demure. She is a woman who knows what she wants and says so.</p>

<p>The church needs this voice. It needs it because the church has spent too long telling women that desire is a male prerogative — that men want and women accommodate, that male desire is natural and female desire is dangerous, that the godly woman is responsive rather than initiating. The Song contradicts this at every turn. The woman in the Song is not responsive. She is ravenous. And the text does not correct her or rebuke her or suggest that her desire is excessive. The text celebrates her. The text gives her the most lines, the most initiative, the most unrestrained expression of longing in the entire biblical canon.</p>

<p>If the church took the Song seriously — if it allowed this woman's voice to shape its theology of desire — it would have to revise a significant portion of what it teaches about women, sexuality, and the godly marriage. It would have to make room for female desire not as a concession but as a gift. It would have to stop treating women's sexuality as a problem to be managed and start treating it as a dimension of the imago Dei to be honored.</p>

<h2>Desire Without Possession</h2>

<p>One of the Song's most theologically rich features is its treatment of desire as something that exists between the lovers but is not owned by either of them. The beloved is present and absent, found and lost, possessed and elusive — and the Song does not resolve this tension. It holds it.</p>

<p>"I opened to my beloved, but my beloved had turned and gone." The woman opens the door and the man has vanished. She searches the streets. She is beaten by the watchmen. She finds him, and then, in the next chapter, she loses him again. The cycle of presence and absence, finding and losing, is the Song's structural principle, and it mirrors something true about desire itself: desire is not satisfied by possession. It is sustained by the interplay of having and wanting, of closeness and distance, of the beloved who is here and the beloved who is gone.</p>

<p>This is a counterintuitive insight, and the church has not known what to do with it. The church's theology of marriage tends toward the language of fulfillment — marriage satisfies desire, completes the person, provides the context in which sexual longing finds its proper resolution. The Song suggests something different. It suggests that desire, even within a committed relationship, retains an element of longing — that the beloved is never fully known, never fully possessed, never reduced to the familiar and the predictable.</p>

<p>This is not a failure of the relationship. It is the nature of desire. And the marriage that makes room for this truth — that allows the spouse to remain, in some sense, a mystery rather than a solved equation — is a marriage that preserves the energy that the Song celebrates. The couple that has been married for thirty years and still finds the other person surprising, still notices something new, still feels the pull of curiosity toward someone they have known for decades — that couple has learned what the Song teaches. Desire does not end with familiarity. It deepens. But only if the marriage preserves the space for the other person to be other — not a possession, not an extension of the self, but a distinct person who remains, at some irreducible level, beyond your reach.</p>

<h2>The Garden</h2>

<p>The Song's central image is the garden. "A garden locked is my sister, my bride, a garden locked, a spring sealed." And then, the invitation: "Awake, O north wind, and come, O south wind. Blow upon my garden, let its spices flow. Let my beloved come to his garden, and eat its choicest fruits."</p>

<p>The garden is the woman's body. It is also more than the woman's body. It is the space of intimacy itself — the enclosed, cultivated, fruitful space where the lovers meet. And the Song's use of the garden deliberately echoes the Garden of Eden — the original garden, the place where the man and the woman were naked and not ashamed.</p>

<p>This echo is theologically profound. The Song is a return to Eden — not the Eden of innocence, but the Eden of intimacy. The Song's lovers are not innocent. They live in a world of watchmen and walls, of loss and searching, of desire that is not always fulfilled. But within the garden — within the enclosed space of their love — they recover something of the original nakedness. They are seen and known and desired, and they are not ashamed.</p>

<p>The church has taught that the Fall ruined sexuality — that shame entered the garden with sin, and that every subsequent experience of sexual desire carries the taint of the Fall. The Song offers a different possibility: that redemption restores desire to something close to its original intention. Not innocent — the Song's lovers are not innocent — but healed. The shame that the Fall introduced can be overcome, within the garden, by a love that sees the body and calls it beautiful, that knows the person and calls them beloved, that desires without possessing and gives without losing.</p>

<h2>What the Church Must Recover</h2>

<p>The church must recover the Song of Solomon. Not as an allegory. Not as a proof text for the goodness of marital sex, trotted out at marriage retreats and then safely shelved. As a central, canonical, authoritative text that shapes the church's theology of desire, bodies, and intimacy.</p>

<p>This recovery would require the church to say several things it has been reluctant to say. That the body is good — not merely tolerable, not merely the container of the soul, but good in itself, worthy of celebration, beautiful in its specificity. That desire is good — not merely permitted within marriage, but good as a dimension of human experience, as an energy created by God, as a force that draws people into the deepest form of knowing another person. That female desire is good — not dangerous, not excessive, not something to be managed or redirected, but good, and deserving of the same space and honor that male desire has always received.</p>

<p>The Song does not resolve the tension between desire and faithfulness. It does not offer a formula for a happy sex life. It does not provide tips or techniques or five steps to a better marriage. What it offers is something far more valuable: a vision. A vision of two people who desire each other without shame, who celebrate each other's bodies without embarrassment, who lose and find each other in a cycle that never quite resolves — and who do all of this in a text that the community of faith has preserved as the word of God.</p>

<p>That the church has spent two thousand years trying to explain away this text is a measure of how far the church has drifted from its own Scripture. The Song is not an anomaly. It is a corrective — a corrective to the body-denying, desire-fearing, pleasure-suspecting tradition that has dominated Christian thought about sexuality since Augustine.</p>

<p>The corrective is in the canon. It has been there all along. The church has only to open the book and read.</p>

<p>And then to stop being embarrassed by what it finds. Because the God who inspired this text is not embarrassed. The God who created desire is not ashamed of desire. And the church that claims to worship this God should not be ashamed of it either.</p>

<p>The garden is open. The invitation stands. The Song is waiting for the church to hear it — not as an allegory, not as an embarrassment, but as the word it has always been: a celebration of the bodies God made and the desire God created and the love that, strong as death, will not let us go.</p>`
